{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Enforce the 50/72 commit-message rule with a local commit-msg hook\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nGerrit renders a change description as preformatted text and preserves the author's newlines, so the wrapping a commit lands with is exactly what reviewers read (and what `git log` shows in an 80-column terminal). Gerrit's bundled `commit-message-length-validator` only WARNS (`warning: subject >50 characters`, `warning: too many message lines longer than 72 characters; manually wrap lines`), which is trivially ignored — and nothing in this repo checked it.\n\nResult: commit bodies on `main` are split between hard-wrapped (72-79 col) and single-paragraph (129-286 col) styles. Measured with the new checker against the last 40 commits on origin/main: 5 pass, 35 fail, with failures like 177, 183, 222 and 223-char body lines. That inconsistency is what made the wrapping look like something rebar was doing to the text (investigated under ticket 368a, closed not_a_bug).\n\nResearch basis — all four major Gerrit-based projects mandate the same rule, and none avoids the line breaks:\n- Go: \"The text should be wrapped at around 72 columns\" + \"Don't use HTML, Markdown, or any other markup language.\"\n- Chromium: \"Long lines should be wrapped to 72 columns for easier log message viewing in terminals.\"\n- ChromiumOS: \"Wrap the body text to 72 characters so that `git log` looks nice\"; subject ~60, 72 hard limit.\n- Android: \"Provide a long description that hard-wraps at 72 characters maximum\"; subject 50 max.\n\n## Approach\n\nEnforce locally at commit time (operator's explicit choice: pre-commit framework, NOT CI).\n\n- `scripts/check_commit_message_wrap.py` — check-only checker for the 50/72 rule, matching the repo's existing `scripts/check_*.py` convention. Never rewrites the message; it names the offending lines.\n- Wired as a `commit-message-wrap` hook in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` at the **commit-msg** stage. It cannot run at the `pre-commit` stage because the commit message does not exist yet. `default_install_hook_types: [pre-commit, commit-msg]` installs both.\n- `make hooks` now verifies BOTH hooks landed AND that Gerrit's Change-Id stamping survived.\n\nCarve-outs (wrapping these would break them): trailers (`rebar-ticket:`, `Change-Id:`, `Signed-off-by:`), `TAG=` lines, fenced code blocks, indented literal blocks, table rows, and lines whose length comes from a single unsplittable atomic token (URL, path, dotted identifier). Comment lines and the `git commit --verbose` diff are ignored.\n\n## Critical risk handled: Gerrit's commit-msg hook\n\nGerrit's own `commit-msg` hook (which stamps `Change-Id`) already owns that slot. `pre-commit install` defaults to MIGRATION mode, preserving the existing hook as `commit-msg.legacy` and running both. Verified empirically in a throwaway repo: a conforming commit still received its `Change-Id`; an over-long body was rejected and no commit was created. `pre-commit install -f/--overwrite` would delete Gerrit's hook and break every push — documented as a do-not-do in the config, the Makefile, and CONTRIBUTING.md, and `make hooks` warns if the Change-Id chain is missing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A commit whose body has a line over 72 columns is REJECTED locally, with output naming each offending line and its length.\n- [ ] A commit whose subject exceeds 50 columns is REJECTED locally.\n- [ ] A conforming commit succeeds AND still receives its Gerrit `Change-Id` trailer (Gerrit's hook is chained as `commit-msg.legacy`, not clobbered).\n- [ ] Trailers (`rebar-ticket:`, `Change-Id:`, `Signed-off-by:`), `TAG=` lines, fenced code blocks, indented literal blocks, table rows, and lines whose length comes from one unsplittable atomic token (URL/path/dotted identifier) are NOT flagged.\n- [ ] git's own scaffolding produces no false positives: comment (`#`) lines and the `git commit --verbose` diff below the `>8` marker are ignored.\n- [ ] The checker never mutates the commit message (check-only, matching the repo's stated pre-commit policy).\n- [ ] `.pre-commit-config.yaml` carries `default_install_hook_types: [pre-commit, commit-msg]` so a bare `pre-commit install` (what `make hooks` runs) installs BOTH hook types.\n- [ ] `make hooks` verifies both hook types landed AND that Change-Id stamping survives in `commit-msg` or `commit-msg.legacy`, failing/warning otherwise.\n- [ ] CONTRIBUTING.md documents the enforcement, the exemption list, why the hook runs at the commit-msg stage, and the never-run-`pre-commit install -f` warning.\n- [ ] Unit tests cover the rule, every carve-out, the fence-toggle case, and the CLI exit codes.\n\n## Notes / scope\n\nLocal guardrail only — bypassable with `git commit --no-verify`, and CI does NOT check wrapping (deliberate: the operator chose pre-commit over CI). Existing history is not rewritten; the gate applies to new commits only."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785277331383700001,"uuid":"05ba9126-c6a2-4bb7-befa-711ac03c1ba8"}